Article 3 The exploitation of the following mineral resources shall be subject to examination and registration by the competent geology and mineral resources department of the State Council, which shall issue a mining license:
(1) Mineral resources within state-planned mining areas and mining areas of significant value to the national economy;
(2) Mineral resources within the territorial sea and other sea areas under Chinese jurisdiction;
(3) Mineral resources exploited with foreign investment;
(4) Mineral resources listed in the appendix to these Measures.
For the exploitation of oil and natural gas minerals, after examination and approval by the organ designated by the State Council, the competent geology and mineral resources department of the State Council shall register and issue a mining license.
The exploitation of the following mineral resources shall be subject to examination and registration by the competent geology and mineral resources department of the people's government of a province, autonomous region, or municipality directly under the Central Government, which shall issue a mining license:
(1) Mineral resources with a medium-scale or larger reserve, other than those specified in the first and second paragraphs of this Article;
(2) Mineral resources that the competent geology and mineral resources department of the State Council has authorized the competent geology and mineral resources department of the people's government of a province, autonomous region, or municipality directly under the Central Government to examine and register.
For the exploitation of mineral resources other than those specified in the first, second, and third paragraphs of this Article, the department responsible for geology and mineral resources management of the local people's government at or above the county level shall, in accordance with the administrative measures formulated by the standing committee of the people's congress of the province, autonomous region, or municipality directly under the Central Government, conduct examination and registration and issue a mining license.
If the mining area crosses the administrative areas of two or more counties, the registration authority at the next higher level common to all the administrative areas involved shall conduct examination and registration and issue a mining license.
After examination and issuance of a license, the department responsible for geology and mineral resources management of the local people's government at or above the county level shall, level by level, report to the department responsible for geology and mineral resources management of the people's government at the next higher level for record.

Article 5 When applying for a mining license, the applicant for a mining right shall submit the following documents to the registration authority:
(1) An application for registration and a map of the mining area;
(2) A certificate of the qualifications of the applicant for a mining right;
(3) A plan for the development and utilization of mineral resources;
(4) An approval document for the establishment of a mining enterprise in accordance with law;
(5) An environmental impact assessment report on the exploitation of mineral resources;
(6) Other documents as prescribed by the competent geology and mineral resources department of the State Council.
If the application is for exploiting mineral resources within state-planned mining areas or mining areas of significant value to the national economy, or for exploiting specific mineral species subject to protective exploitation by the state, the applicant shall also submit an approval document from the relevant competent department of the State Council.
If the application is for exploiting oil or natural gas, the applicant shall also submit the approval document of the State Council for the establishment of an oil company or for consent to the exploitation of oil or natural gas, as well as a certificate of the legal person qualification of the mining enterprise.

Article 12 In any of the following circumstances, upon application by the mining right holder, subject to examination and approval by the registration authority of the people's government at or above the provincial level in accordance with the measures for reduction or exemption of mining right usage fees and consideration for mining rights formulated by the competent geology and mineral resources department of the State Council jointly with the finance department of the State Council, the mining right usage fee and consideration for the mining right may be reduced or exempted:
(1) Exploitation of mineral resources in remote and impoverished areas;
(2) Exploitation of mineral species in short supply in the state;
(3) Heavy losses or suspension of operations of the mining enterprise due to force majeure such as natural disasters;
(4) Other circumstances prescribed by the competent geology and mineral resources department of the State Council and the finance department of the State Council.

Article 15 In any of the following circumstances, the mining right holder shall, within the term of validity of the mining license, apply to the registration authority for modification registration:
(1) Change of the mining area;
(2) Change of the main mineral species exploited;
(3) Change of the exploitation method;
(4) Change of the name of the mining enterprise;
(5) Transfer of the mining right approved according to law.
